---
// Product cards share one structure: descriptor, summary, three points, closing note.
// Copy is condensed from Karthik's supplied text — every claim and caveat is his; nothing new.
// Status chips are intentionally omitted for now.
---
<section class="section" id="products">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="eyebrow">Our products</span>
    <h2>Instruments for quality that <em>holds up</em>.</h2>

    <div class="products-grid">
      <article class="product-card">
        <div class="product-head">
          <h3>Qualgraph</h3>
          <a class="card-link" href="/qualgraph">Learn more →</a>
        </div>
        <p class="product-tag">Evidence-backed quality reviews.</p>
        <p class="product-sum">Checks recorded application behavior against agreed rules and brings supported evidence from across your tools into one clear review.</p>
        <ul class="card-list">
          <li>Shows what was checked, what was found and where more evidence is needed</li>
          <li>Answers what you have verified, where the gaps are and what to check next</li>
          <li>Retains reviewed findings and quality history to compare results over time</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="card-note"><span>Decisions</span> AI assists with analysis and explanation. People remain responsible for decisions.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="product-card">
        <div class="product-head">
          <h3>Warrant</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="product-tag">Oversight for AI decisions.</p>
        <p class="product-sum">For AI systems that make or recommend decisions: shows how reliable the checks are, and what the AI is authorized to do.</p>
        <ul class="card-list">
          <li>Measures how closely an AI checker agrees with your experts, and whether that changes when the model changes</li>
          <li>Records the AI’s scope of permission, who approved it, when it expires and the supporting evidence</li>
          <li>Gives auditors a record they can review independently</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="card-note"><span>Ownership</span> The organization runs Warrant and owns the results.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="product-card">
        <div class="product-head">
          <h3>Reality Lab</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="product-tag">Realistic, controlled testing.</p>
        <p class="product-sum">Shows how software behaves in realistic situations, including when things go wrong, with evidence teams can inspect.</p>
        <ul class="card-list">
          <li>Runs defined scenarios and records what happened</li>
          <li>Helps teams understand failures and check fixes</li>
          <li>Provides a place to test Qualgraph, Warrant and the checks themselves</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="card-note"><span>Today</span> Limited to controlled development testing. Broader enterprise simulation and independent certification remain future capabilities.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="who">
      <span class="eyebrow">Who it's for</span>
      <h3 class="who-title">Built for the people who own the <em>quality bar</em>.</h3>
      <div class="aud-grid">
        <div class="aud">
          <div class="role">QA &amp; Quality Leaders</div>
          <h3>Prove the bar is real.</h3>
          <p>Move past "we tested it" to evidence you can defend — where decisions held, where they slipped, and what the signals were telling you all along.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="aud">
          <div class="role">Engineering Leaders</div>
          <h3>Ship fast without shipping blind.</h3>
          <p>Keep velocity while making every sign-off traceable. When something gets through, you can reconstruct exactly how — and close the gap.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
